/* Microbiology 8: Parasitology */
Z("micro8",0,"m",["Entamoeba histolytica is an intestinal protozoan that causes amoebic dysentery and liver abscess; it spreads by eating cysts in contaminated food and water.","The cyst is a sealed survival capsule passed in stool; the trophozoite is the active invader that eats red cells and burrows into the colon wall, making flask-shaped ulcers.",["Forms: trophozoite (invasive, ingests RBCs, single nucleus with central karyosome) and cyst (infective; mature quadrinucleate, chromatoid bars).","Life cycle: ingestion of mature cysts → excystation in ileum → trophozoites in colon → encystation → cysts in stool; single host (man).","Pathogenesis: adhesion (Gal/GalNAc lectin), amoebapores, cysteine proteases → flask-shaped ulcers (caecum, sigmoid); spread via portal vein → liver abscess (right lobe, anchovy sauce pus).","Lab: stool microscopy (trophozoites with ingested RBCs, cysts), antigen ELISA, PCR (distinguishes E. dispar), serology (extraintestinal), culture (NIH, Robinson medium)."],"Amoeba: 'Flask ulcer, Anchovy sauce, RBC-eating trophozoite'."],
"Entamoeba histolytica is a pathogenic intestinal amoeba causing amoebiasis. Morphology: trophozoite (15–20 µm; active, with finger-like pseudopodia showing purposeful, directional motility; clear ectoplasm and granular endoplasm containing ingested RBCs — diagnostic of E. histolytica; single spherical nucleus with fine peripheral chromatin and a small central karyosome — 'cartwheel'); precyst; cyst (10–15 µm; spherical, refractile wall; one to four nuclei — mature quadrinucleate cyst is infective; chromatoid bodies with rounded ends and glycogen mass in young cysts). Culture: polyxenic (Boeck–Drbohlav, NIH, Robinson medium) and axenic (TYI-S-33). Life cycle: single host — humans; infection by ingestion of mature quadrinucleate cysts in faecally contaminated food or water (or sexual oral–anal route); excystation in the small intestine (ileum) releases a metacyst that divides into eight trophozoites; they colonise the large intestine (caecum, ascending colon, rectosigmoid), multiply by binary fission, and either invade or encyst; cysts pass in stool (carriers are main source). Pathogenesis: trophozoites adhere to colonic epithelium by Gal/GalNAc lectin, kill cells via amoebapores and contact-dependent cytolysis, and degrade matrix with cysteine proteases; they invade through mucosa, spread laterally in submucosa forming flask-shaped ulcers with narrow neck and broad base, with minimal inflammation; amoeboma (granulomatous mass), perforation, haemorrhage; haematogenous spread through portal vein to liver forming amoebic liver abscess (right lobe, single; chocolate-brown 'anchovy sauce' pus without neutrophils, trophozoites at periphery), then lung, brain, skin. Clinical: asymptomatic cyst passers (most), amoebic dysentery (gradual onset, large offensive stools with blood and mucus, abdominal pain, little fever), chronic amoebic colitis, amoeboma, amoebic liver abscess (fever, right upper quadrant pain, tender hepatomegaly; may rupture into pleura, lung, pericardium, peritoneum), cutaneous amoebiasis. Laboratory diagnosis: stool — macroscopic (blood, mucus, offensive odour), saline and iodine mounts for trophozoites (with RBCs) and cysts, Charcot–Leyden crystals, few pus cells; repeated (three) samples; concentration methods; stool antigen ELISA (Gal/GalNAc lectin, specific for E. histolytica), PCR (distinguishes non-pathogenic E. dispar and E. moshkovskii); culture; colonoscopy with biopsy; liver abscess — ultrasound, serology (ELISA, IHA — high titres in invasive disease), aspirate (trophozoites rarely). Treatment: metronidazole/tinidazole followed by luminal agent (diloxanide furoate, paromomycin).",
[["Morphology",[["Feature","Trophozoite","Cyst"],["Size","15–20 µm","10–15 µm"],["Motility","Active, directional pseudopodia","Non-motile"],["Nucleus","Single, central karyosome","1–4 nuclei (mature: 4)"],["Inclusions","Ingested RBCs (diagnostic)","Chromatoid bars, glycogen"],["Role","Invasive, pathogenic","Infective, transmission"]]],
["Life cycle",["Ingestion of mature quadrinucleate cyst","Excystation in ileum → 8 trophozoites","Colonisation of colon; binary fission","Invasion (ulcers) or encystation","Cysts passed in faeces → infect new host"]],
["Pathogenesis and lesions",["Gal/GalNAc lectin adhesion; amoebapores; cysteine proteases","Flask-shaped ulcers (caecum, rectosigmoid)","Amoeboma; perforation; haemorrhage","Portal spread → liver abscess (right lobe, anchovy sauce)","Extension to lung, pleura, pericardium, brain, skin"]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["Stool microscopy: trophozoites with RBCs, cysts (saline/iodine), Charcot–Leyden crystals","Stool antigen ELISA; PCR (vs E. dispar)","Culture: NIH, Robinson medium","Serology for extraintestinal amoebiasis","Ultrasound/CT for liver abscess"]]],
{i:"Amoebiasis affects about 50 million people annually and is common in India due to poor sanitation; only E. histolytica among intestinal amoebae is clearly pathogenic.",f:"Ingestion of mature 4-nucleate cyst → excystation (ileum)\n → trophozoites in colon → invasion → flask-shaped ulcer → dysentery\n → portal vein → liver abscess (anchovy sauce)\n → encystation → cysts in stool → transmission",c:"E. histolytica is identified by trophozoites containing RBCs or specific antigen/PCR tests; treatment requires a tissue amoebicide followed by a luminal agent to eradicate cysts."});

Z("micro8",1,"m",["Amoebic dysentery (Entamoeba histolytica) and bacillary dysentery (Shigella) both cause bloody diarrhoea but differ in onset, stool appearance and cells in the stool.","Bacillary dysentery is a sudden violent attack with many inflammatory cells; amoebic dysentery is a slower siege with offensive stools and few inflammatory cells.",["Bacillary: acute, fever, frequent small stools of blood and mucus without faecal matter, odourless; many pus cells, macrophages.","Amoebic: gradual, afebrile, fewer large offensive stools with blood mixed in faeces; few pus cells, trophozoites with RBCs, Charcot–Leyden crystals.","Bacillary: alkaline; amoebic: acidic stool reaction.","Treatment: bacillary — antibiotics (ciprofloxacin, azithromycin); amoebic — metronidazole + luminal agent."],"Amoebic = 'Acidic, Afebrile, Amoebae with RBCs, Charcot–Leyden'."],
"Dysentery is diarrhoea with blood and mucus. Amoebic dysentery is caused by Entamoeba histolytica, bacillary dysentery by Shigella (also EIEC, Campylobacter, Salmonella). They can be distinguished clinically and by stool examination. Bacillary dysentery has acute onset with fever, toxaemia, severe tenesmus and frequent (10–20/day) small stools consisting mainly of blood and mucus with little faecal matter, odourless; reaction alkaline; microscopy shows numerous pus cells, macrophages (may resemble amoebae), and RBCs in rouleaux, no Charcot–Leyden crystals; culture yields Shigella. Amoebic dysentery has insidious onset, mild or no fever, less tenesmus, fewer (6–8/day) large offensive stools with blood and mucus mixed with faeces; reaction acidic; microscopy shows few pus cells, RBCs in clumps, Charcot–Leyden crystals and trophozoites of E. histolytica with ingested RBCs; ulcers are flask-shaped vs superficial in bacillary.",
[["Differences",[["Feature","Amoebic","Bacillary"],["Organism","E. histolytica","Shigella"],["Onset","Gradual","Acute"],["Fever","Absent/mild","High"],["Tenesmus","Less","Marked"],["Number of stools","6–8/day","> 10/day"],["Amount","Large","Small"],["Odour","Offensive","Odourless"],["Character","Blood and mucus mixed with faeces","Blood and mucus, little faeces"],["Reaction","Acidic","Alkaline"],["Pus cells","Few","Numerous"],["RBCs","In clumps","Discrete, rouleaux"],["Macrophages","Few","Many"],["Charcot–Leyden crystals","Present","Absent"],["Parasite","Trophozoites with ingested RBCs","—"],["Ulcers","Flask-shaped, deep","Superficial, irregular"]]]],
{c:"Stool examination readily separates amoebic from bacillary dysentery, which require different treatments."});

Z("micro8",2,"m",["Giardia lamblia (intestinalis) is a pear-shaped flagellate that lives in the small intestine and causes diarrhoea and malabsorption, spread by contaminated water.","A tiny 'face'-shaped parasite with two eyes (nuclei) and a sucking disc that sticks to the gut lining like a suction cup, blocking absorption.",["Trophozoite: pear/tennis-racket shaped, bilaterally symmetrical, 2 nuclei, 4 pairs of flagella, sucking disc — 'falling leaf' motility.","Cyst: oval, 4 nuclei, infective; resistant to chlorine.","Disease: steatorrhoea, foul greasy stools, abdominal cramps, malabsorption (fat, vitamins, lactose); worse in IgA deficiency.","Lab: stool microscopy (cysts, trophozoites), string test (Enterotest), duodenal aspirate, antigen ELISA/IF, PCR. Treatment: metronidazole, tinidazole."],"Giardia = 'Grins at you (face), Greasy stools, Gulped from water'."],
"Giardia lamblia (G. intestinalis, G. duodenalis) is a flagellate protozoan inhabiting the duodenum and upper jejunum. Trophozoite: pear- or tennis-racket-shaped (10–20 µm), bilaterally symmetrical, with two nuclei, a pair of axostyles, two median bodies, four pairs of flagella and a concave ventral sucking disc for adhesion; it shows 'falling leaf' motility; stained appearance resembles a face. Cyst: oval (10–12 µm), thick-walled, with four nuclei (mature), axostyles and fibrils; infective form, resistant to chlorination. Life cycle: single host; ingestion of cysts in contaminated water or food (also person to person, day care, oral–anal); excystation in duodenum; trophozoites attach to mucosa, multiply by binary fission and encyst in colon; cysts passed in stools. Pathogenesis: attachment by sucking disc, mechanical barrier, villous atrophy, disaccharidase deficiency, bacterial overgrowth; no invasion. Clinical: asymptomatic carriage, acute diarrhoea, chronic diarrhoea with foul-smelling, greasy, pale, bulky stools (steatorrhoea), flatulence, abdominal distension, weight loss, malabsorption of fat, vitamin A, B12, lactose intolerance; growth retardation in children; severe in hypogammaglobulinaemia and IgA deficiency; travellers' diarrhoea. Laboratory diagnosis: stool examination (saline and iodine mounts; repeated samples as excretion is intermittent) for cysts and trophozoites; concentration techniques; duodenal aspirate or string test (Enterotest); duodenal biopsy; stool antigen ELISA, DFA, PCR. Treatment: metronidazole, tinidazole, nitazoxanide.",
[["Morphology",[["Feature","Trophozoite","Cyst"],["Shape","Pear-shaped, bilaterally symmetrical","Oval"],["Nuclei","2","4 (mature)"],["Flagella","4 pairs","Retained as fibrils"],["Special","Sucking disc, falling-leaf motility","Infective, chlorine resistant"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["Stool microscopy (3 samples): cysts, trophozoites","String test / duodenal aspirate","Antigen ELISA, DFA, PCR","Duodenal biopsy"]]],
{c:"Giardiasis is a common cause of waterborne diarrhoea and malabsorption; repeated stool examination or antigen testing establishes the diagnosis."});

Z("micro8",3,"m",["Trichomonas vaginalis is a flagellated protozoan that causes a sexually transmitted infection — vaginitis in women and urethritis in men.","A jerky swimmer with flagella and an undulating membrane that irritates the vagina, causing frothy discharge and a 'strawberry' cervix.",["Only trophozoite stage (no cyst); pear-shaped, 4 anterior flagella, undulating membrane, axostyle; jerky motility.","Transmission: sexual; survives on moist objects briefly.","Women: frothy yellow-green foul discharge, itching, strawberry cervix, pH > 4.5; men: often asymptomatic, urethritis.","Lab: wet mount (motile trophozoites), culture (Feinberg–Whittington, InPouch), antigen tests, NAAT. Treat both partners with metronidazole."],"Trichomonas = 'Trophozoite only, frothy discharge, strawberry cervix, treat partner'."],
"Trichomonas vaginalis is a flagellate protozoan causing trichomoniasis, the commonest non-viral sexually transmitted infection. It exists only as a trophozoite (no cyst): pear-shaped (10–30 µm) with a single nucleus, four anterior flagella, a fifth recurrent flagellum forming the outer edge of an undulating membrane extending half the body, and an axostyle projecting posteriorly; it shows jerky, twitching motility. It inhabits the vagina and cervix in women and the urethra and prostate in men. Transmission is sexual; neonates may acquire during delivery. Pathogenesis: adhesion to epithelium, contact-dependent cytotoxicity, proteases; vaginal pH rises above 4.5 as lactobacilli fall. Clinical: in women — vaginitis with profuse, frothy, yellow-green, foul-smelling discharge, pruritus, dyspareunia, dysuria, punctate haemorrhages on cervix ('strawberry cervix'); associated with preterm birth, low birth weight, increased HIV transmission; in men — usually asymptomatic or mild urethritis, prostatitis. Laboratory diagnosis: wet mount of vaginal discharge or urethral/prostatic secretion (motile trophozoites; sensitivity 50–70%), stained smears (Giemsa, Papanicolaou), culture (Diamond's, Feinberg–Whittington, InPouch TV — gold standard before NAAT), rapid antigen tests, NAAT (most sensitive), whiff test negative or positive, raised pH. Treatment: metronidazole or tinidazole for both partners.",
[["Features",["Trophozoite only; no cyst","4 anterior flagella + undulating membrane + axostyle","Jerky motility","Sexually transmitted"]],
["Diagnosis",["Wet mount: motile trophozoites","Culture: Feinberg–Whittington, InPouch","Antigen tests, NAAT","Vaginal pH > 4.5"]]],
{c:"Trichomoniasis is easily diagnosed by wet mount or NAAT and cured with metronidazole, treating both partners to prevent reinfection."});

Z("micro8",4,"m",["Leishmania donovani is a protozoan spread by sandfly bites that infects macrophages of spleen, liver and bone marrow, causing kala-azar (visceral leishmaniasis).","The parasite turns the body's own garbage collectors (macrophages) into homes, filling the spleen until it becomes enormous.",["Forms: amastigote (LD body — in human macrophages, oval with nucleus and kinetoplast) and promastigote (flagellated, in sandfly gut and culture).","Vector: female Phlebotomus argentipes (sandfly); in India anthroponotic (humans only reservoir); Bihar, Jharkhand, West Bengal, UP.","Kala-azar: prolonged fever (double rise), massive splenomegaly, hepatomegaly, pancytopenia, hypergammaglobulinaemia, darkening of skin; PKDL after treatment.","Lab: rK39 rapid test, LD bodies in splenic (most sensitive) / marrow aspirate, NNN culture, aldehyde test (Napier), PCR."],"Kala-azar: 'Sandfly, Spleen massive, rK39, LD bodies'."],
"Leishmania donovani (complex) is a haemoflagellate causing visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar). Morphology: amastigote (LD body; 2–4 µm, oval, intracellular in macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system; nucleus and rod-shaped kinetoplast; no external flagellum) in humans; promastigote (15–25 µm, spindle-shaped, with anterior flagellum and kinetoplast) in sandfly gut and culture. Culture: NNN (Novy–MacNeal–Nicolle) medium at 22–24 °C yields promastigotes; also Schneider's medium. Life cycle: digenetic — vertebrate host (humans; in India anthroponotic, with no animal reservoir; dogs elsewhere) and vector female sandfly Phlebotomus argentipes. Sandfly ingests amastigotes with blood meal; they transform into promastigotes in the midgut, multiply and migrate to the pharynx/proboscis (infective metacyclic promastigotes); when it bites, promastigotes are inoculated, phagocytosed by macrophages, transform into amastigotes and multiply, spreading to spleen, liver, bone marrow and lymph nodes. Pathogenesis: proliferation of macrophages and RE system → massive splenomegaly, hepatomegaly; marrow replacement → pancytopenia; polyclonal B-cell activation → hypergammaglobulinaemia, reversed A:G ratio; immunosuppression (Th2) → secondary infections; skin hyperpigmentation ('kala-azar' = black fever). Clinical: incubation 2–6 months; prolonged irregular fever (sometimes double rise in 24 h), weight loss, massive splenomegaly, hepatomegaly, lymphadenopathy, anaemia, bleeding, darkening of skin; death from secondary infections. Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis (PKDL): hypopigmented macules or nodules on face and body months–years after treatment; important reservoir. HIV co-infection worsens disease. Laboratory diagnosis: parasitological — amastigotes (LD bodies) in Giemsa/Leishman-stained aspirates of spleen (sensitivity > 95%; risk of bleeding), bone marrow (60–85%), lymph node, buffy coat; culture in NNN medium; PCR; serology — rK39 immunochromatographic rapid test (used in Indian programme), direct agglutination test, IFAT, ELISA; non-specific — pancytopenia, raised globulins, aldehyde (Napier) test and Chopra antimony test (positive due to hypergammaglobulinaemia; historical); leishmanin (Montenegro) skin test negative during active disease, positive after cure. Treatment: single-dose liposomal amphotericin B (India), miltefosine, paromomycin; sodium stibogluconate (resistance in Bihar). Control: indoor residual spraying, case detection, elimination programme.",
[["Morphology",[["Form","Host","Features"],["Amastigote (LD body)","Human macrophages","Oval, nucleus + kinetoplast, no flagellum"],["Promastigote","Sandfly gut, NNN culture","Spindle, anterior flagellum"]]],
["Life cycle",["Sandfly bite inoculates promastigotes","Macrophages engulf → amastigotes multiply","Spread to spleen, liver, marrow","Sandfly ingests amastigotes → promastigotes in midgut → proboscis"]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",[["Test","Comment"],["rK39 RDT","Rapid, used in Indian programme"],["Splenic aspirate","Most sensitive (> 95%), bleeding risk"],["Bone marrow aspirate","Safer, 60–85%"],["NNN culture","Promastigotes"],["PCR","Sensitive"],["DAT, ELISA","Serology"],["Aldehyde test","Non-specific (globulins)"]]],
["Clinical features",["Prolonged fever (double rise)","Massive splenomegaly, hepatomegaly","Pancytopenia, hypergammaglobulinaemia","Hyperpigmentation","PKDL after treatment"]]],
{i:"India has made major progress towards eliminating kala-azar, which is concentrated in Bihar, Jharkhand, West Bengal and eastern UP.",f:"Sandfly bite → promastigotes → macrophages → amastigotes (LD bodies)\n → RE system (spleen, liver, marrow) → splenomegaly, pancytopenia, ↑ globulins\n → sandfly ingests amastigotes → promastigotes (midgut) → proboscis",c:"Kala-azar is diagnosed by rK39 tests and demonstration of LD bodies; single-dose liposomal amphotericin B and vector control underpin the elimination programme."});

Z("micro8",5,"m",["Chagas disease (American trypanosomiasis) is caused by Trypanosoma cruzi, spread by faeces of triatomine ('kissing') bugs; it damages the heart and gut years later.","The kissing bug bites and defecates; the parasite in the faeces enters through the bite or eye and hides in heart and gut muscle, slowly damaging them.",["Vector: reduviid/triatomine bug; transmission also by transfusion, transplacental, oral.","Acute: chagoma at bite, Romaña sign (unilateral eyelid oedema).","Chronic: cardiomyopathy (arrhythmias, apical aneurysm), megaoesophagus, megacolon (destruction of myenteric plexus).","Lab: acute — trypomastigotes in blood (C-shaped); chronic — serology, xenodiagnosis, PCR. Treatment: benznidazole, nifurtimox. Found in Latin America, not India."],"Chagas = 'C-shaped, Cardiomyopathy, Colon & oesophagus megas'."],
"Chagas disease is caused by Trypanosoma cruzi, a haemoflagellate endemic in Central and South America. Forms: trypomastigote (in blood; C- or U-shaped with large posterior kinetoplast and undulating membrane; does not multiply in blood), amastigote (intracellular in muscle and other cells; multiplies) and epimastigote (in vector gut). Vector: triatomine (reduviid, 'kissing') bugs that bite at night and defecate; metacyclic trypomastigotes in faeces enter through the bite wound or conjunctiva; other routes — blood transfusion, organ transplant, congenital, oral (contaminated food). Clinical: acute phase — local chagoma, Romaña sign (painless unilateral periorbital oedema), fever, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, myocarditis, meningoencephalitis; indeterminate phase; chronic phase (10–30 years later in 30%) — dilated cardiomyopathy with conduction defects, arrhythmias, apical aneurysm, thromboembolism, sudden death; megaoesophagus and megacolon due to destruction of autonomic ganglia. Diagnosis: acute — thick and thin blood films, buffy coat, culture (NNN), PCR; chronic — serology (ELISA, IFAT), xenodiagnosis (laboratory-reared bugs fed on patient), PCR. Treatment: benznidazole, nifurtimox (effective mainly in acute phase). Contrast: T. brucei (African sleeping sickness, tsetse fly).",
[["Features",["Organism: Trypanosoma cruzi","Vector: triatomine bug (faecal contamination)","Acute: chagoma, Romaña sign, myocarditis","Chronic: cardiomyopathy, megaoesophagus, megacolon","Diagnosis: blood film (acute), serology, xenodiagnosis, PCR"]]],
{c:"Chagas disease is a major cause of cardiomyopathy in Latin America; screening of blood donors and vector control are key preventive measures."});

Z("micro8",6,"m",["Plasmodium is the malaria parasite, transmitted by female Anopheles mosquitoes; it multiplies first in the liver and then in red cells, causing cyclical fever, anaemia and, with P. falciparum, life-threatening complications.","The parasite has a two-home lifestyle: sexual life in the mosquito, asexual life in humans — first hiding in the liver, then bursting red cells in waves that cause fever paroxysms.",["Species: P. falciparum (malignant), P. vivax (commonest in India; relapses — hypnozoites), P. ovale, P. malariae, P. knowlesi.","Human cycle: sporozoites → liver (exoerythrocytic schizogony) → merozoites → RBCs (ring → trophozoite → schizont) → gametocytes. Mosquito: gametocytes → gametes → zygote → ookinete → oocyst → sporozoites (sporogony).","Clinical: paroxysms — cold, hot, sweating stages; tertian (vivax, ovale, falciparum 48 h) and quartan (malariae 72 h); anaemia, splenomegaly.","Lab: thick and thin Giemsa/Leishman smears (gold standard), RDTs (HRP-2 for falciparum, pLDH), QBC, PCR."],"Malaria cycle: 'Sporozoite → Liver → RBC → Gametocyte → Mosquito'."],
"Malaria is caused by protozoa of genus Plasmodium: P. vivax (most widespread; relapses), P. falciparum (most severe; malignant tertian), P. malariae (quartan), P. ovale (relapses; Africa) and P. knowlesi (zoonotic, South-East Asia). In India P. vivax and P. falciparum predominate. Life cycle: definitive host — female Anopheles mosquito (sexual cycle, sporogony); intermediate host — humans (asexual cycle, schizogony). In humans: sporozoites inoculated by the mosquito reach the liver within 30 minutes, invade hepatocytes and undergo pre-erythrocytic (exoerythrocytic) schizogony (5–15 days) producing thousands of merozoites; in P. vivax and P. ovale some sporozoites remain dormant as hypnozoites causing relapses. Merozoites invade RBCs (P. vivax — reticulocytes via Duffy antigen; P. falciparum — RBCs of all ages) and undergo erythrocytic schizogony: ring form → trophozoite → schizont → merozoites released on RBC rupture every 48 h (vivax, ovale, falciparum) or 72 h (malariae), causing fever paroxysms; some merozoites develop into male and female gametocytes. In the mosquito: gametocytes ingested with blood → exflagellation → microgamete fertilises macrogamete → zygote → motile ookinete → oocyst on midgut wall → sporozoites migrate to salivary glands (extrinsic incubation 10–14 days). Pathogenesis: RBC rupture releases pyrogens (haemozoin, GPI) → TNF, IL-1 → fever; haemolysis and dyserythropoiesis → anaemia; splenomegaly; in falciparum — knobs (PfEMP1) cause cytoadherence, rosetting and sequestration in microvasculature (brain, kidney, placenta, lungs) → severe malaria. Clinical: incubation 9–14 days (falciparum shorter); paroxysms with cold stage (rigors), hot stage (high fever) and sweating stage, recurring every 48 or 72 h; anaemia, splenomegaly, hepatomegaly, jaundice; recrudescence (falciparum, malariae) and relapse (vivax, ovale). Laboratory diagnosis: peripheral smear — thick film (screening, parasite density) and thin film (species identification) stained with Giemsa, Leishman or JSB, taken preferably during fever; rapid diagnostic tests — HRP-2 (P. falciparum-specific), pLDH (pan-specific and species-specific), aldolase; quantitative buffy coat (acridine orange); PCR (species, low parasitaemia); serology (epidemiology). Supporting: anaemia, thrombocytopenia. Treatment (NVBDCP): vivax — chloroquine + primaquine 14 days (G6PD test); falciparum — ACT (artesunate + sulfadoxine–pyrimethamine; artemether–lumefantrine in NE) + single dose primaquine; severe malaria — IV artesunate.",
[["Species comparison",[["Feature","P. vivax","P. falciparum","P. malariae","P. ovale"],["Fever cycle","48 h (tertian)","36–48 h (malignant tertian)","72 h (quartan)","48 h"],["RBC infected","Reticulocytes, enlarged","All ages, not enlarged","Old RBCs","Young, oval, fimbriated"],["Stippling","Schüffner dots","Maurer clefts","Ziemann dots","James dots"],["Smear forms","All stages","Rings and gametocytes (crescent) only","All; band form","All"],["Hypnozoites","Yes (relapse)","No","No","Yes"],["Severity","Benign (can be severe)","Severe","Nephrotic syndrome","Benign"]]],
["Life cycle",[["Phase","Host","Process"],["Pre-erythrocytic schizogony","Human liver","Sporozoites → merozoites"],["Erythrocytic schizogony","Human RBC","Ring → trophozoite → schizont → merozoites (fever)"],["Gametogony","Human RBC","Gametocytes formed"],["Sporogony","Anopheles","Gametes → zygote → ookinete → oocyst → sporozoites"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",[["Test","Use"],["Thick smear","Detection, parasite count"],["Thin smear","Species identification"],["RDT (HRP-2, pLDH)","Rapid field diagnosis"],["QBC","Fluorescent detection"],["PCR","Species, mixed and low-density infections"]]]],
{i:"Malaria remains endemic in parts of India (Odisha, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, NE states); the national goal is elimination by 2030.",x:[["Relapse vs recrudescence",["Relapse: reactivation of liver hypnozoites (vivax, ovale) — prevented by primaquine","Recrudescence: persistence of blood forms (falciparum, malariae) due to inadequate treatment or resistance"]]],f:"Mosquito bite → sporozoites → liver (schizogony; hypnozoites in vivax/ovale)\n → merozoites → RBC: ring → trophozoite → schizont → rupture (fever every 48/72 h)\n → gametocytes → mosquito: gametes → zygote → ookinete → oocyst → sporozoites (salivary glands)",c:"Understanding the Plasmodium life cycle explains periodic fever, relapses and the targets of antimalarial drugs; smear microscopy and RDTs remain central to diagnosis."});

Z("micro8",7,"m",["P. falciparum causes severe ('malignant') malaria because infected red cells stick to blood vessel walls, blocking circulation in brain, kidneys, lungs and placenta.","Infected red cells grow sticky knobs and jam the body's smallest roads, causing organ failure.",["Cerebral malaria: coma, seizures; sequestration in brain capillaries.","Severe anaemia, hypoglycaemia (also from quinine), lactic acidosis, ARDS/pulmonary oedema.","Acute kidney injury, blackwater fever (massive haemolysis → haemoglobinuria), jaundice, DIC, shock (algid malaria).","Pregnancy: abortion, low birth weight, placental malaria. Treatment: IV artesunate."],"Severe falciparum 'Brain, Blood, Blackwater, Breath, Baby'."],
"Plasmodium falciparum causes most severe malaria and deaths because it invades RBCs of all ages producing high parasitaemia and expresses PfEMP1 on knobs on the RBC surface, causing cytoadherence to endothelium (ICAM-1, CD36, EPCR), rosetting and sequestration of mature parasites in microvessels, impairing perfusion; cytokine release and endothelial activation contribute. Complications (WHO criteria for severe malaria): cerebral malaria (impaired consciousness, coma, convulsions; brain swelling, ring haemorrhages, Dürck granulomas), severe anaemia (Hb < 7 g/dL adults, < 5 children), hypoglycaemia (parasite consumption, impaired gluconeogenesis, quinine-induced hyperinsulinaemia; especially in pregnancy and children), metabolic (lactic) acidosis, acute kidney injury (ATN), blackwater fever (massive intravascular haemolysis with haemoglobinuria, often after quinine in partially immune), acute respiratory distress/pulmonary oedema, circulatory collapse (algid malaria; Gram-negative septicaemia), jaundice and hepatic dysfunction, abnormal bleeding and DIC, hyperparasitaemia (> 5%), splenic rupture. In pregnancy — severe anaemia, hypoglycaemia, abortion, stillbirth, preterm birth, low birth weight (placental sequestration via CSA). Tropical splenomegaly syndrome (hyperreactive malarial splenomegaly) in chronic exposure. Management: IV/IM artesunate, supportive care.",
[["Complications",[["System","Complication"],["CNS","Cerebral malaria — coma, seizures"],["Blood","Severe anaemia, DIC, thrombocytopenia"],["Kidney","AKI, blackwater fever"],["Lung","Pulmonary oedema, ARDS"],["Metabolic","Hypoglycaemia, lactic acidosis"],["CVS","Algid malaria (shock)"],["Liver","Jaundice"],["Pregnancy","Abortion, LBW, maternal anaemia"]]]],
{c:"P. falciparum's cytoadherence and sequestration explain its life-threatening complications; early recognition and IV artesunate reduce mortality."});

Z("micro8",8,"m",["Toxoplasma gondii is a protozoan whose definitive host is the cat; humans are infected by eating undercooked meat or cat-contaminated food, and it is dangerous in pregnancy and in immunocompromised people.","The cat is the parasite's home base; humans are accidental guests who swallow tissue cysts or oocysts and may harbour lifelong dormant cysts.",["Forms: tachyzoite (rapidly multiplying, crescent-shaped), bradyzoite in tissue cysts (dormant in brain, muscle), oocyst (in cat faeces).","Transmission: undercooked meat (tissue cysts), oocysts (soil, vegetables), transplacental (primary infection in pregnancy), transfusion/transplant.","Congenital: chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, intracranial calcification (diffuse), convulsions (Sabin tetrad); adults mostly lymphadenopathy; AIDS — encephalitis with ring-enhancing lesions.","Lab: serology (IgM, IgG, IgG avidity), PCR (amniotic fluid, CSF), Sabin–Feldman dye test (historical), histology, mouse inoculation."],"Toxo: 'Cat → oocyst; Meat → cyst; Mother → fetus'."],
"Toxoplasma gondii is an obligate intracellular coccidian protozoan. Forms: tachyzoite (trophozoite; crescentic, 4–8 µm, rapidly multiplying in any nucleated cell during acute infection), tissue cyst containing bradyzoites (slowly multiplying, persist lifelong in brain, eye, skeletal and cardiac muscle; basis of latent infection) and oocyst (in cat faeces; sporulates in soil within 1–5 days to become infective). Life cycle: definitive hosts — cats and felines (enteroepithelial sexual cycle producing oocysts); intermediate hosts — humans, sheep, pigs, rodents, birds (extraintestinal asexual cycle). Human infection: ingestion of tissue cysts in undercooked meat (pork, lamb), ingestion of sporulated oocysts from soil, cat litter, contaminated vegetables and water; transplacental transmission of tachyzoites during primary maternal infection; blood transfusion, organ transplantation. Pathogenesis: tachyzoites multiply, destroying cells; host CMI (IFN-γ) controls infection, converting to bradyzoites in tissue cysts; reactivation with immunosuppression. Clinical: in immunocompetent — mostly asymptomatic or lymphadenopathy (cervical) with mononucleosis-like illness, chorioretinitis; congenital toxoplasmosis (risk of transmission increases, severity decreases with gestational age) — abortion, stillbirth, Sabin tetrad (chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus or microcephaly, diffuse intracranial calcifications, convulsions), jaundice, hepatosplenomegaly, later learning disability and visual loss; immunocompromised — cerebral toxoplasmosis in AIDS (CD4 < 100; multiple ring-enhancing lesions), pneumonitis, myocarditis, disseminated disease in transplant recipients. Laboratory diagnosis: serology — IgM and IgG ELISA, IgG avidity (high avidity excludes infection in previous 4 months — important in pregnancy), IgA, Sabin–Feldman dye test (gold standard historically), indirect IF; PCR on amniotic fluid (fetal infection), CSF, blood, aqueous humour; histology — tachyzoites and cysts (Giemsa, IHC), lymph node — Piringer–Kuchinka triad; isolation by mouse inoculation or cell culture; imaging. Treatment: pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine + folinic acid; spiramycin in pregnancy; co-trimoxazole prophylaxis in HIV.",
[["Forms",[["Form","Where","Significance"],["Tachyzoite","Acute infection, any nucleated cell","Rapid multiplication; transplacental"],["Bradyzoite (tissue cyst)","Brain, eye, muscle","Latent infection; meat-borne transmission"],["Oocyst","Cat faeces","Environmental transmission"]]],
["Transmission routes",["Undercooked meat (tissue cysts)","Oocysts in soil, cat litter, vegetables, water","Transplacental (primary maternal infection)","Transfusion, organ transplant"]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",[["Test","Use"],["IgM, IgG ELISA","Acute vs past infection"],["IgG avidity","Timing in pregnancy"],["PCR (amniotic fluid, CSF)","Fetal and CNS infection"],["Histology","Tachyzoites, cysts"],["Sabin–Feldman dye test","Reference (historical)"]]]],
{i:"About a third of the world's population is infected with Toxoplasma; disease is significant mainly in pregnancy and immunosuppression.",f:"Cat (sexual cycle) → oocysts in faeces → soil/food\n → intermediate hosts (pigs, sheep) → tissue cysts in meat\n → human ingestion → tachyzoites → tissue cysts (latent)\n Pregnancy (primary) → fetus; AIDS → reactivation (encephalitis)",c:"Toxoplasmosis is usually silent but dangerous in pregnancy and immunodeficiency; serology with avidity testing and PCR guide diagnosis and management."});

Z("micro8",9,"m",["Taenia solium (pork tapeworm) and Taenia saginata (beef tapeworm) are human tapeworms; T. solium is more dangerous because its eggs can cause cysticercosis, including neurocysticercosis.","Both worms are long ribbons in the gut, but the pork worm's eggs can hatch inside humans and form cysts in the brain — like seeds sprouting in the wrong place.",["T. solium: scolex with hooks (armed) and 4 suckers; gravid segment with 7–13 lateral uterine branches; intermediate host pig.","T. saginata: unarmed scolex (no hooks); 15–30 uterine branches; intermediate host cattle; segments actively motile.","Eggs identical (radially striated); differentiate by scolex and gravid proglottid.","Cysticercosis (T. solium eggs) → neurocysticercosis — commonest cause of acquired epilepsy in India; diagnosis CT/MRI, serology (EITB)."],"Solium = 'Spiky (hooks), Swine, Seizures'; Saginata = 'Smooth, Steak'."],
"Taenia solium and Taenia saginata are cestodes whose adult worms live in the human small intestine (definitive host). T. solium (pork tapeworm) is 2–3 m long; scolex globular with four suckers and a rostellum armed with a double row of hooks; gravid proglottids have 7–13 lateral branches of uterus each side, are expelled passively in chains; intermediate host is the pig (cysticercus cellulosae in muscles — 'measly pork'); humans also act as intermediate host by ingesting eggs (contaminated food, water, faecal–oral autoinfection), leading to cysticercosis — cysticerci in subcutaneous tissue, muscles, eye and brain (neurocysticercosis: seizures, raised intracranial pressure, hydrocephalus; commonest cause of acquired epilepsy in developing countries). T. saginata (beef tapeworm) is longer (5–10 m); scolex quadrate with four suckers, no rostellum or hooks (unarmed); gravid segments have 15–30 lateral uterine branches, are actively motile and may crawl out of the anus singly; intermediate host is cattle (cysticercus bovis); human cysticercosis does not occur. Eggs of both are identical (spherical, 30–40 µm, thick radially striated embryophore enclosing hexacanth oncosphere). Infection by eating undercooked infected meat; intestinal taeniasis causes mild abdominal symptoms. Diagnosis: eggs in stool or perianal swab (cannot speciate), gravid proglottids (uterine branches by India ink injection), scolex after treatment, coproantigen ELISA, PCR; neurocysticercosis — CT/MRI (cysts with scolex 'dot', calcifications), EITB serology. Treatment: praziquantel, niclosamide; albendazole with steroids for neurocysticercosis.",
[["Differences",[["Feature","T. solium","T. saginata"],["Common name","Pork tapeworm","Beef tapeworm"],["Length","2–3 m","5–10 m"],["Scolex","Globular, rostellum with hooks (armed)","Quadrate, no hooks (unarmed)"],["Proglottids","< 1000","1000–2000"],["Gravid uterus branches","7–13","15–30"],["Expulsion of segments","Passive, in chains","Active, singly"],["Intermediate host","Pig","Cattle"],["Larva","Cysticercus cellulosae","Cysticercus bovis"],["Human cysticercosis","Yes","No"],["Eggs","Identical","Identical"]]]],
{c:"Distinguishing the two tapeworms matters because only T. solium causes cysticercosis; hygiene, meat inspection and thorough cooking prevent both."});

Z("micro8",10,"m",["A hydatid cyst is the larval stage of the dog tapeworm Echinococcus granulosus in humans, forming slow-growing fluid-filled cysts mostly in the liver and lungs. The Casoni test was an old skin test for it.","The dog is the worm's home; humans accidentally swallow eggs from dog faeces and become 'dead-end' hosts in which water-filled balloons grow for years.",["Definitive host: dog; intermediate: sheep (natural), humans (accidental).","Cyst wall: outer host fibrous layer (pericyst), laminated layer (ectocyst), germinal layer (endocyst) → brood capsules, scolices ('hydatid sand'), daughter cysts.","Sites: liver (right lobe, 60–70%), lung (20%), others.","Casoni test: intradermal hydatid fluid → wheal in 20 min (immediate hypersensitivity); obsolete (poor specificity). Diagnosis now: USG/CT, ELISA, immunoblot; avoid aspiration (anaphylaxis)."],"Hydatid = 'Dog egg → sheep/man cyst → hydatid sand'."],
"Hydatid disease (cystic echinococcosis) is caused by the larval stage (metacestode) of Echinococcus granulosus. Adult worm (3–6 mm, three or four proglottids) lives in the small intestine of dogs (definitive host); eggs passed in dog faeces are ingested by sheep, cattle (intermediate hosts) or accidentally by humans (dead-end hosts) through contaminated hands, food or water. Oncospheres hatch in the duodenum, penetrate the intestinal wall, enter portal circulation and lodge in liver (60–70%; first filter), lungs (20–25%), and less commonly brain, bone, kidney, spleen, orbit, where they develop slowly into hydatid cysts. Cyst structure: pericyst (host fibrous capsule), ectocyst (acellular laminated hyaline membrane), endocyst (germinal layer producing brood capsules containing protoscolices); fluid is clear, antigenic; detached brood capsules and protoscolices form 'hydatid sand'; daughter cysts may form inside. Dogs are infected by eating offal of infected sheep. Clinical: often asymptomatic for years; pressure effects (hepatomegaly, RUQ pain, cough, haemoptysis), rupture causing anaphylaxis and secondary hydatidosis, biliary obstruction, infection. Casoni test: intradermal injection of 0.2 mL sterile hydatid fluid produces a wheal with pseudopodia within 20 minutes (immediate hypersensitivity) in infected persons; positive for years; obsolete because of low specificity and risk of sensitisation. Diagnosis: imaging (ultrasound — cysts with daughter cysts, water-lily sign of detached membrane on CT/X-ray), serology (ELISA, IHA, immunoblot for antigen 5 and B), microscopy of fluid from surgically removed cysts (hooklets, scolices), eosinophilia. Avoid diagnostic aspiration unless PAIR under cover. Treatment: albendazole, PAIR (puncture–aspiration–injection–re-aspiration), surgery. Prevention: deworming dogs, preventing dogs from eating offal.",
[["Cyst structure",[["Layer","Nature"],["Pericyst","Host fibrous tissue"],["Ectocyst","Laminated acellular membrane (parasite)"],["Endocyst","Germinal layer — brood capsules, scolices"],["Contents","Hydatid fluid, hydatid sand, daughter cysts"]]],
["Diagnosis",["Ultrasound/CT — cyst with daughter cysts, water-lily sign","Serology: ELISA, IHA, immunoblot","Eosinophilia","Casoni test (obsolete)"]]],
{c:"Hydatid disease is a zoonosis linked to dogs and sheep; imaging and serology diagnose it, and careful surgical or PAIR management avoids anaphylaxis from spillage."});

Z("micro8",11,"m",["Ascaris lumbricoides (roundworm) is the largest intestinal nematode of humans; its eggs are swallowed from contaminated soil, and its larvae pass through the lungs before growing into adults in the small intestine.","The worm takes a tourist trip: hatched in the gut, it travels through blood to the lungs (causing cough), climbs up the windpipe, is swallowed again and finally settles in the gut.",["Adult: large (15–35 cm), creamy pink; male tail curved.","Eggs: fertilised (bile-stained, mammillated, round), unfertilised (elongated, irregular); decorticated forms.","Life cycle: single host; embryonated eggs ingested → larvae hatch → portal → liver → heart → lungs (Loeffler syndrome) → trachea → swallowed → adults in jejunum. Eggs need 2–3 weeks in soil to embryonate.","Complications: intestinal obstruction (worm bolus), biliary/pancreatic duct obstruction, appendicitis, malnutrition, Loeffler pneumonitis. Diagnosis: eggs in stool; treatment albendazole."],"Ascaris route 'Gut → Liver → Lung → Gut'."],
"Ascaris lumbricoides is the largest common intestinal nematode of humans. Adult: elongated, cylindrical, creamy-white or pinkish; female 20–35 cm with straight tail; male 15–30 cm with ventrally curved tail and two copulatory spicules; mouth with three lips. The female lays about 200,000 eggs daily. Eggs: fertilised — round or oval (60 × 45 µm), bile-stained brown, thick shell with outer mammillated albuminous coat, containing an unsegmented ovum, float in saturated salt solution; unfertilised — elongated (90 × 45 µm), thin shell, irregular coat, filled with refractile granules, do not float; decorticated eggs lack the albuminous coat. Life cycle: single host (humans); eggs passed in faeces embryonate in moist warm soil in 2–3 weeks (rhabditiform larva inside — infective); ingestion with contaminated food, water or hands; larvae hatch in the small intestine, penetrate the mucosa, enter portal circulation, reach liver, then right heart and lungs (day 4–5), moult twice in alveoli, ascend the bronchi and trachea, are swallowed, and mature into adults in the jejunum in 6–8 weeks (lung migration — 'Loss' cycle). Pathogenesis and clinical: larval migration — Loeffler syndrome (eosinophilic pneumonitis: cough, wheeze, fever, transient infiltrates, eosinophilia), allergic manifestations; adult worms — malnutrition (competition for nutrients, malabsorption), vague abdominal pain; mechanical complications from wandering worms or bolus — intestinal obstruction, intussusception, volvulus, perforation, appendicitis, biliary colic, obstructive jaundice, cholangitis, liver abscess, acute pancreatitis; worms may be vomited or passed per rectum or nose. Laboratory diagnosis: stool microscopy (saline/iodine) for eggs; concentration (formol-ether, salt flotation); Kato–Katz quantitation; adult worms passed; larvae in sputum during pulmonary phase; eosinophilia; imaging (barium, ultrasound — worms in bile duct). Treatment: albendazole 400 mg single dose, mebendazole, pyrantel pamoate, ivermectin. Prevention: sanitation, hygiene, mass deworming (National Deworming Day).",
[["Eggs",[["Feature","Fertilised","Unfertilised"],["Shape","Round/oval","Elongated, irregular"],["Size","60 × 45 µm","90 × 45 µm"],["Shell","Thick, mammillated","Thin, irregular coat"],["Contents","Unsegmented ovum","Refractile granules"],["Floats in salt","Yes","No"]]],
["Life cycle",["Embryonated egg ingested","Larva hatches in small intestine","Portal vein → liver → right heart → lungs","Alveoli → bronchi → trachea → swallowed","Adults in jejunum; eggs in faeces (embryonate in soil 2–3 weeks)"]],
["Complications",["Loeffler syndrome (larval pneumonitis)","Malnutrition, growth retardation","Intestinal obstruction (worm bolus), volvulus, intussusception","Biliary obstruction, cholangitis, liver abscess, pancreatitis","Appendicitis, perforation"]]],
{i:"Ascariasis affects over 800 million people worldwide; children in areas with poor sanitation are most affected.",f:"Embryonated egg (soil) → ingested → larva in gut\n → portal → liver → heart → lungs (Loeffler)\n → trachea → swallowed → adult in jejunum → eggs in stool\n → soil (embryonate 2–3 wk)",c:"Ascaris is diagnosed by eggs in stool; its migrating larvae and wandering adults explain both pulmonary and surgical complications, and mass deworming controls it."});

Z("micro8",12,"m",["Hookworms (Ancylostoma duodenale and Necator americanus) are small intestinal worms whose larvae penetrate bare skin from soil; adults suck blood from the gut wall, causing iron-deficiency anaemia.","Tiny vampires attached to the intestinal wall with teeth, drinking blood day and night; they enter through bare feet on contaminated soil.",["A. duodenale: buccal capsule with 2 pairs of teeth; N. americanus: cutting plates; male copulatory bursa.","Eggs: oval, thin-shelled, colourless (not bile-stained), segmented ovum (4–8 blastomeres), float in salt.","Life cycle: eggs in soil → rhabditiform → filariform larva (infective) → penetrates skin → lungs → swallowed → adults in jejunum. A. duodenale also oral and transmammary.","Disease: ground itch, Loeffler pneumonitis, microcytic hypochromic anaemia (0.2 mL blood/worm/day for A. duodenale), hypoproteinaemia; diagnosis eggs in stool, Kato–Katz; Charcot–Leyden crystals; treatment albendazole + iron."],"Hookworm = 'Hooks on, Hb off' (iron deficiency anaemia)."],
"Hookworms are blood-sucking nematodes of the small intestine; Ancylostoma duodenale (Old World hookworm) and Necator americanus (New World; also common in South India). Adults are small (1 cm), greyish-white, with a buccal capsule — A. duodenale has two pairs of ventral teeth and a C-shaped body; N. americanus has two semilunar cutting plates and an S-shaped body; males have a copulatory bursa. Eggs: oval (60 × 40 µm), colourless (not bile-stained), thin transparent shell with clear space, segmented ovum with 4–8 blastomeres; float in saturated salt. Life cycle: single host; eggs passed in faeces hatch in warm moist shaded soil into rhabditiform larvae within 1–2 days, which moult into infective filariform (third-stage) larvae in 5–8 days; these penetrate intact skin (feet of barefoot walkers, hands of agricultural workers); A. duodenale also by ingestion and possibly transmammary route; larvae travel via venous blood to the right heart and lungs, break into alveoli, ascend the respiratory tract, are swallowed and mature in the jejunum attaching to the mucosa; eggs appear in faeces in 5–7 weeks. Pathogenesis and clinical: skin penetration — ground itch (pruritic papulovesicular dermatitis); larval migration — Loeffler-like pneumonitis; intestinal phase — epigastric pain, dyspepsia, diarrhoea, pica; chronic blood loss (A. duodenale ~0.15–0.2 mL/worm/day; N. americanus ~0.03 mL) causes iron-deficiency microcytic hypochromic anaemia, hypoproteinaemia with oedema, fatigue, breathlessness, heart failure, growth and cognitive retardation in children, adverse pregnancy outcomes; Wakana disease (oral infection with A. duodenale). Laboratory diagnosis: stool microscopy for eggs (fresh stool — eggs hatch on standing; differentiate from Strongyloides larvae), concentration (salt flotation, formol-ether), egg counts (Stoll, Kato–Katz) to assess intensity; occult blood in stool; Charcot–Leyden crystals; blood — microcytic hypochromic anaemia, eosinophilia, low ferritin; Harada–Mori culture to differentiate larvae. Treatment: albendazole/mebendazole, iron supplementation, nutritional support. Prevention: sanitary latrines, wearing footwear, mass deworming.",
[["Species comparison",[["Feature","A. duodenale","N. americanus"],["Shape","C-shaped","S-shaped"],["Buccal capsule","2 pairs of teeth","2 cutting plates"],["Blood loss/worm/day","0.15–0.2 mL","0.03 mL"],["Routes","Skin, oral, transmammary","Skin"],["Eggs/day","10,000–30,000","5,000–10,000"]]],
["Life cycle",["Eggs in faeces → soil → rhabditiform larva → filariform (infective)","Skin penetration → veins → heart → lungs","Alveoli → trachea → swallowed","Adults attach to jejunal mucosa, suck blood"]],
["Clinical and lab",["Ground itch, pneumonitis","Iron deficiency anaemia, hypoproteinaemia","Stool: thin-shelled segmented eggs; egg counts","Eosinophilia, occult blood, Charcot–Leyden crystals"]]],
{i:"Hookworm infection is a leading cause of iron-deficiency anaemia in rural India, especially among children and pregnant women.",f:"Egg (faeces) → soil → rhabditiform → filariform larva\n → skin penetration (bare feet) → blood → lungs → trachea → swallowed\n → adult in jejunum (teeth) → blood loss → iron-deficiency anaemia",c:"Hookworm disease is a soil-transmitted helminthiasis whose main effect is chronic blood loss; footwear, sanitation and deworming with iron supplementation control it."});

Z("micro8",13,"m",["The NIH swab is a cellophane-tipped glass rod used to collect pinworm (Enterobius vermicularis) eggs from the perianal skin early in the morning.","Pinworm females lay eggs at night around the anus; the sticky cellophane picks them up like a lint roller.",["Enterobius eggs are laid on perianal skin at night, not in stool.","Swab taken early morning before bath or defecation.","Cellophane (sticky side out) rolled over perianal skin, then examined under microscope.","Eggs: planoconvex (D-shaped), colourless, embryonated; adhesive tape (Scotch tape/Graham) method is similar."],"NIH swab = 'Night-laid eggs, Is collected at morning, Has cellophane'."],
"The NIH (National Institutes of Health) swab is the method of choice for diagnosis of enterobiasis (threadworm/pinworm infection). It consists of a glass rod with a piece of transparent cellophane attached at one end by a rubber band, kept in a test tube. Gravid female Enterobius vermicularis migrate out of the anus at night and deposit eggs on perianal skin, so eggs are rarely found in stool (5%). Procedure: swabbing is done early in the morning before the patient bathes or defecates; the cellophane end is rubbed over the perianal folds, then removed, placed on a slide with a drop of toluene or saline and examined under the microscope for characteristic eggs — planoconvex (flattened on one side, D-shaped), colourless, non-bile-stained, double-walled, containing a tadpole-shaped larva. Swabs on three consecutive mornings detect most infections. The adhesive cellophane tape (Graham's Scotch tape) method is a simpler alternative. Adult worms may also be seen on perianal skin or in stools. Clinical: perianal pruritus especially at night, disturbed sleep, irritability, vulvovaginitis in girls; autoinfection common. Treatment: albendazole/mebendazole single dose repeated after 2 weeks for whole family.",
[["Procedure",["Early morning, before bathing/defecation","Cellophane end rubbed over perianal skin","Place on slide with toluene/saline","Examine for D-shaped embryonated eggs","Repeat on 3 consecutive days"]]],
{c:"The NIH swab or adhesive tape test is essential for diagnosing pinworm infection because eggs are deposited on the perianal skin, not in faeces."});

Z("micro8",14,"m",["Wuchereria bancrofti is a filarial worm transmitted by Culex mosquitoes; adult worms live in lymphatics causing lymphangitis, hydrocele and elephantiasis, and their microfilariae circulate in blood at night.","The adult worms block the body's drainage pipes (lymphatics), causing massive swelling of legs and scrotum; their babies (microfilariae) appear in the blood at night when mosquitoes bite.",["Vector: Culex quinquefasciatus (India); man is definitive host, mosquito intermediate host.","Microfilariae: sheathed, nocturnal periodicity (10 pm–4 am), no nuclei in tail tip.","Pathology: lymphatic dilatation, lymphangitis, lymphadenitis, hydrocele, chyluria, elephantiasis; tropical pulmonary eosinophilia.","Lab: night blood smear (thick, Giemsa), DEC provocation test, membrane filtration, antigen test (ICT, Og4C3), ultrasound 'filarial dance sign', PCR."],"W. bancrofti: 'Sheathed, Night, Culex, Elephant legs'."],
"Wuchereria bancrofti is a filarial nematode causing lymphatic filariasis. Adult worms are thread-like, creamy-white (female 8–10 cm, male 4 cm) and live coiled in lymphatic vessels and nodes. Females are viviparous, releasing microfilariae (250–300 µm × 8 µm) which are sheathed (sheath stains poorly with Giemsa, pink with haematoxylin), have smooth curves, a short cephalic space and no nuclei at the tail tip, and show nocturnal periodicity (peak in peripheral blood between 10 pm and 4 am), aligned with the night-biting vector. Life cycle: definitive host — humans; intermediate host and vector — female Culex quinquefasciatus mosquito (in India; Aedes, Anopheles elsewhere). Mosquito ingests microfilariae with blood meal; they shed sheaths, penetrate the stomach wall, migrate to thoracic muscles, develop through sausage-shaped first stage and second stage to infective third-stage (L3) larvae in 10–14 days, which migrate to the proboscis; during a bite L3 larvae enter skin through the puncture, reach lymphatics, and mature into adults over 6–12 months. Pathogenesis: live adult worms cause lymphatic dilatation (lymphangiectasia) via toxins and immune responses; dying worms trigger inflammation, granulomas and fibrosis with lymphatic obstruction; recurrent bacterial infections aggravate lymphoedema. Clinical: asymptomatic microfilaraemia; acute — filarial fever, adenolymphangitis (retrograde), funiculitis, epididymo-orchitis; chronic — hydrocele (commonest in India), lymphoedema progressing to elephantiasis of legs, scrotum, arms, breasts, vulva; chyluria; tropical pulmonary eosinophilia (occult filariasis; immune hyper-response to microfilariae in lungs — nocturnal cough, wheeze, very high eosinophilia, high IgE, no microfilariae in blood). Laboratory diagnosis: microscopy of night blood (thick and thin smears stained with Giemsa or haematoxylin; wet mount for motility), concentration methods (Knott's, membrane filtration — Nuclepore), DEC provocation test (100 mg DEC brings microfilariae into day blood); antigen detection (ICT card for circulating filarial antigen, Og4C3 ELISA — day or night); antibody tests; PCR; ultrasound showing 'filarial dance sign' of live adults in scrotal lymphatics; lymphoscintigraphy; biopsy (rarely). Treatment and control: DEC, albendazole, ivermectin; mass drug administration (DEC + albendazole ± ivermectin) under the National Programme to Eliminate Lymphatic Filariasis; vector control; morbidity management (limb hygiene, hydrocelectomy).",
[["Microfilaria features",["Sheathed","Nuclear column ends before tail tip (no tail nuclei)","Smooth graceful curves","Nocturnal periodicity (10 pm–4 am)"]],
["Life cycle",["Culex bite deposits L3 larvae","Larvae → lymphatics → adults (6–12 months)","Females release microfilariae → blood (nocturnal)","Mosquito ingests microfilariae → L1 → L2 → L3 in thoracic muscles (10–14 days) → proboscis"]],
["Clinical spectrum",[["Stage","Features"],["Asymptomatic","Microfilaraemia"],["Acute","Filarial fever, lymphangitis, lymphadenitis, epididymo-orchitis"],["Chronic","Hydrocele, lymphoedema, elephantiasis, chyluria"],["Occult","Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",[["Test","Comment"],["Night blood smear","Thick film, Giemsa"],["DEC provocation","Daytime sampling"],["Membrane filtration / Knott's","Concentration"],["ICT / Og4C3 antigen","Any time of day"],["USG filarial dance sign","Live adult worms"],["PCR","Sensitive"]]]],
{i:"India carries a large share of the global lymphatic filariasis burden; mass drug administration aims at elimination.",f:"Culex bite → L3 larvae → lymphatics → adult worms\n → microfilariae in blood (night) → mosquito → L1 → L2 → L3 → proboscis\nAdults: lymphangiectasia → inflammation (dead worms) → obstruction → lymphoedema, hydrocele, elephantiasis",c:"Lymphatic filariasis is diagnosed by night blood smears or antigen tests; elimination relies on mass drug administration and vector control, with morbidity management for chronic disease."});

Z("micro8",15,"m",["Helminths can be grouped by how they reproduce and where they live: some lay eggs, some give birth to larvae; some eggs float in salt solution and some are bile-stained; and some larvae wander under the skin or in organs (larva migrans).","These are 'sorting lists' that help remember which worm does what — handy for exam enumerations.",["Viviparous (larvae born): Trichinella, Wuchereria, Brugia, Dracunculus. Oviparous (eggs): Ascaris, Trichuris, Ancylostoma, Enterobius. Ovo-viviparous (eggs hatch at once): Strongyloides.","Intestinal parasites: Entamoeba, Giardia, Cryptosporidium, Ascaris, hookworm, Trichuris, Enterobius, Strongyloides, Taenia, H. nana.","Larva migrans: cutaneous (Ancylostoma braziliense — dog/cat hookworm), visceral (Toxocara canis), ocular (Toxocara), neural (Angiostrongylus).","Bile-stained eggs: Ascaris, Trichuris, Taenia, Clonorchis, Fasciola, H. nana (not); non-bile-stained: hookworm, Enterobius, H. nana. Eggs that do not float: unfertilised Ascaris, Taenia, Fasciola, Schistosoma, Clonorchis, larvae of Strongyloides."],"Viviparous 'TWDB' = Trichinella, Wuchereria, Dracunculus, Brugia."],
"Nematodes may be classified by mode of reproduction: viviparous (give birth to larvae) — Trichinella spiralis, Wuchereria bancrofti, Brugia malayi, Dracunculus medinensis, Loa loa, Onchocerca; oviparous (lay eggs) — Ascaris lumbricoides, Trichuris trichiura, Ancylostoma duodenale, Necator americanus, Enterobius vermicularis; ovo-viviparous (lay eggs containing larvae that hatch immediately) — Strongyloides stercoralis. Parasites infecting the intestine: protozoa — Entamoeba histolytica, Giardia, Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, Cystoisospora, Balantidium; nematodes — Ascaris, hookworms, Trichuris, Enterobius, Strongyloides, Trichinella (adults); cestodes — Taenia solium, T. saginata, Hymenolepis nana, Diphyllobothrium latum; trematodes — Fasciolopsis buski. Larva migrans: cutaneous larva migrans (creeping eruption) — larvae of animal hookworms (Ancylostoma braziliense, A. caninum) wandering in skin; visceral larva migrans — Toxocara canis/cati larvae in liver, lungs, brain (eosinophilia, hepatomegaly); ocular larva migrans — Toxocara (mimics retinoblastoma); neural larva migrans — Angiostrongylus cantonensis, Baylisascaris; also Gnathostoma (larva currens: Strongyloides). Bile-stained eggs (brown): Ascaris, Trichuris, Taenia, Clonorchis, Opisthorchis, Fasciola, Diphyllobothrium, Hymenolepis diminuta; non-bile-stained: hookworm, Enterobius, Hymenolepis nana, Strongyloides. Eggs that float in saturated salt solution: hookworm, fertilised Ascaris, Trichuris, Enterobius, H. nana; eggs that do not float (heavy or operculated): unfertilised Ascaris, Taenia, Clonorchis, Fasciola, Diphyllobothrium (operculated), Schistosoma, and larvae of Strongyloides.",
[["Reproduction",[["Type","Examples"],["Viviparous","Trichinella, Wuchereria, Brugia, Dracunculus, Loa loa, Onchocerca"],["Oviparous","Ascaris, Trichuris, hookworm, Enterobius"],["Ovo-viviparous","Strongyloides"]]],
["Larva migrans",[["Type","Parasite"],["Cutaneous","Ancylostoma braziliense, A. caninum (dog/cat hookworm)"],["Visceral","Toxocara canis, T. cati"],["Ocular","Toxocara"],["Neural","Angiostrongylus cantonensis, Baylisascaris"]]],
["Eggs",[["Property","Examples"],["Bile-stained","Ascaris, Trichuris, Taenia, Clonorchis, Fasciola"],["Non-bile-stained","Hookworm, Enterobius, H. nana"],["Float in salt","Hookworm, fertilised Ascaris, Trichuris, Enterobius, H. nana"],["Do not float","Unfertilised Ascaris, Taenia, operculated (Fasciola, Clonorchis, Diphyllobothrium), Schistosoma"]]]],
{c:"Classifying helminths by reproduction, habitat and egg characteristics aids rapid recall and laboratory identification."});

Z("micro8",16,"m",["Cryptosporidium and Cyclospora are tiny intestinal coccidian parasites that cause watery diarrhoea — self-limited in healthy people but chronic and severe in AIDS.","Tiny oocysts in water resist chlorine; they live just under the gut cell surface and cause profuse watery diarrhoea.",["Cryptosporidium parvum/hominis: oocysts 4–6 µm, modified acid-fast; infective when passed; resistant to chlorine; waterborne outbreaks.","Cyclospora cayetanensis: oocysts 8–10 µm, variable acid-fast, autofluorescent; need days in environment to sporulate; fresh produce.","AIDS (CD4 < 100): chronic, life-threatening diarrhoea.","Diagnosis: modified ZN stain, antigen tests, PCR; treatment nitazoxanide (Cryptosporidium), co-trimoxazole (Cyclospora)."],"Crypto = 4–6 µm; Cyclo = 8–10 µm (double size, autofluorescent)."],
"Cryptosporidium (C. hominis, C. parvum) is an intracellular but extracytoplasmic coccidian parasite of intestinal epithelium. Oocysts (4–6 µm) are passed fully sporulated (infective immediately, allowing autoinfection and person-to-person spread), contain four sporozoites, are resistant to chlorine and are transmitted through contaminated water (swimming pools, municipal supplies), food, animals (zoonotic — calves) and person to person. It causes acute self-limited watery diarrhoea in immunocompetent hosts (especially children, outbreaks), and chronic, profuse, life-threatening cholera-like diarrhoea, malabsorption and biliary disease (cholangitis) in AIDS (CD4 < 100). Diagnosis: modified (cold) ZN stain (1% H2SO4) of stool — pink-red oocysts 4–6 µm; auramine; DFA; stool antigen ELISA/ICT; PCR (multiplex panels); biopsy. Treatment: nitazoxanide, ART for AIDS. Cyclospora cayetanensis forms oocysts of 8–10 µm passed unsporulated, requiring 1–2 weeks in the environment to become infective (no direct person-to-person transmission); linked to contaminated fresh produce (berries, lettuce) and water; causes prolonged watery diarrhoea with fatigue, anorexia, weight loss, relapsing course; severe in HIV. Diagnosis: modified ZN (variable acid-fast staining — 'ghost' oocysts), autofluorescence (blue under UV), safranin stain, PCR. Treatment: co-trimoxazole.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Cryptosporidium","Cyclospora"],["Oocyst size","4–6 µm","8–10 µm"],["Sporulated when passed","Yes (infective)","No (needs days)"],["Person-to-person spread","Yes","No"],["Modified ZN","Uniformly acid-fast","Variable"],["Autofluorescence","No","Yes"],["Source","Water, animals","Fresh produce, water"],["Treatment","Nitazoxanide","Co-trimoxazole"]]]],
{c:"These coccidian parasites cause waterborne and foodborne diarrhoea, severe in AIDS; modified acid-fast staining and molecular tests detect them."});

Z("micro8",17,"m",["Strongyloides stercoralis is a small intestinal nematode that can multiply inside the host (autoinfection), causing lifelong infection and, in immunosuppressed people (especially on steroids), overwhelming hyperinfection.","A worm that can restart its life cycle without leaving the body — normally quiet, but if the immune guard falls (steroids), it multiplies explosively.",["Larvae (not eggs) in stool — rhabditiform larvae with short buccal cavity and prominent genital primordium.","Filariform larvae penetrate skin; free-living cycle in soil possible.","Autoinfection → chronic infection for decades; larva currens (fast-moving urticarial rash).","Hyperinfection/disseminated strongyloidiasis with steroids, HTLV-1: Gram-negative sepsis, meningitis. Diagnosis: stool larvae (Baermann, agar plate culture), serology, PCR; treatment ivermectin."],"Strongyloides = 'Larvae in stool, Larva currens, Lethal with steroids'."],
"Strongyloides stercoralis (threadworm) has a complex life cycle with parasitic and free-living generations. Parasitic females (2 mm) live embedded in the duodenal and jejunal mucosa, reproduce parthenogenetically and lay eggs that hatch in the mucosa; rhabditiform larvae (short buccal cavity, prominent genital primordium) are passed in stool — eggs are rarely seen. In soil, rhabditiform larvae either develop directly into infective filariform larvae (direct cycle) or into free-living adults that produce further generations (indirect cycle). Filariform larvae penetrate skin, migrate via blood to lungs, ascend the airways, are swallowed and mature in the small intestine. Autoinfection: rhabditiform larvae transform into filariform larvae within the gut and re-penetrate the intestinal mucosa (internal) or perianal skin (external), maintaining infection for decades after leaving endemic areas. Clinical: often asymptomatic; skin — larva currens (rapidly migrating serpiginous urticarial tracks around buttocks); pulmonary — Loeffler-like; intestinal — epigastric pain, diarrhoea, malabsorption; eosinophilia. Hyperinfection syndrome and disseminated strongyloidiasis occur in immunosuppressed (corticosteroids most important, HTLV-1, transplant, malignancy, malnutrition): massive larval invasion of lungs, gut and other organs, carrying enteric bacteria leading to Gram-negative sepsis and meningitis; high mortality. Diagnosis: repeated stool examination for larvae, concentration (formol-ether), Baermann technique, Harada–Mori filter paper culture, agar plate culture (tracks), duodenal aspirate, sputum in hyperinfection, serology (ELISA), PCR; eosinophilia (may be absent in hyperinfection). Treatment: ivermectin (drug of choice), albendazole. Screen and treat before starting steroids in patients from endemic areas.",
[["Key features",["Larvae in stool, not eggs","Parthenogenetic females in duodenal mucosa","Free-living cycle in soil","Autoinfection → persistent infection","Larva currens"]],
["Hyperinfection",["Triggers: corticosteroids, HTLV-1, transplant, malignancy","Massive larval migration → Gram-negative sepsis, meningitis, pneumonia","High mortality; treat with ivermectin"]]],
{c:"Strongyloides is unique in its autoinfection cycle; screening and treatment before immunosuppression prevent fatal hyperinfection."});

Z("micro8",18,"m",["Enterobius vermicularis (pinworm) and Trichuris trichiura (whipworm) are common intestinal nematodes: pinworm causes night-time anal itching; whipworm lives in the large intestine and can cause dysentery and rectal prolapse.","Pinworm: a night-time visitor laying eggs around the anus. Whipworm: a worm shaped like a whip, threading its thin end into the colon lining.",["Enterobius: small white worm; eggs planoconvex, embryonated, on perianal skin; NIH swab/tape test; autoinfection; whole family treated.","Trichuris: whip-shaped (thin anterior, thick posterior); eggs barrel/lemon-shaped with bipolar plugs, bile-stained.","Trichuris: direct life cycle without lung migration; heavy infection → dysentery, anaemia, rectal prolapse (children).","Treatment: albendazole/mebendazole."],"Trichuris egg = 'barrel with two corks'; Enterobius egg = 'D-shaped'."],
"Enterobius vermicularis (pinworm, threadworm) is a small white nematode (female 8–13 mm with pointed tail; male 2–5 mm) inhabiting the caecum and appendix. Gravid females migrate out of the anus at night to lay eggs on perianal skin; eggs are planoconvex (flattened on one side), colourless, non-bile-stained, thick-walled, containing a tadpole-shaped larva, infective within hours. Transmission: anus-to-mouth autoinfection via fingers (scratching), contaminated fomites, inhalation of airborne eggs, retroinfection (larvae hatching on perianal skin re-entering the anus). Common in children and families. Clinical: nocturnal perianal pruritus, disturbed sleep, irritability, excoriation, secondary infection; vulvovaginitis, urinary tract infection, salpingitis and appendicitis rarely. Diagnosis: NIH swab or adhesive tape test in early morning; adult worms on perianal skin or stool. Treatment: albendazole/mebendazole/pyrantel, repeated after 2 weeks, treat all household members, hygiene. Trichuris trichiura (whipworm) adults (3–5 cm) have a long thin anterior three-fifths (threaded into the mucosa of caecum and colon) and a thick posterior part (whip-like). Eggs: barrel- or lemon-shaped (50 × 25 µm), bile-stained brown, with transparent mucus plugs at each pole, unsegmented when passed; embryonate in soil in 3–4 weeks. Life cycle: single host; ingestion of embryonated eggs → larvae hatch in small intestine → mature in caecum without lung migration. Clinical: usually asymptomatic; heavy infection in children — Trichuris dysentery syndrome (chronic bloody mucoid diarrhoea, tenesmus), iron-deficiency anaemia, growth retardation, finger clubbing, rectal prolapse (with worms visible on mucosa); appendicitis. Diagnosis: eggs in stool (Kato–Katz quantitation), proctoscopy. Treatment: albendazole, mebendazole.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Enterobius","Trichuris"],["Common name","Pinworm, threadworm","Whipworm"],["Habitat","Caecum, appendix","Caecum, colon"],["Egg","Planoconvex, colourless, embryonated","Barrel-shaped, bile-stained, bipolar plugs"],["Eggs found","Perianal skin (NIH swab)","Stool"],["Transmission","Autoinfection, fomites","Ingestion of embryonated eggs from soil"],["Lung migration","No","No"],["Key clinical","Nocturnal pruritus ani","Dysentery, rectal prolapse, anaemia"]]]],
{c:"Pinworm and whipworm have direct life cycles without lung migration; perianal sampling diagnoses pinworm, stool microscopy whipworm, and both respond to benzimidazoles."});

Z("micro8",19,"m",["Echinococcus granulosus has a two-host life cycle: adult tapeworm in the dog's intestine, larval hydatid cyst in sheep (or humans accidentally).","Dogs eat sheep offal with cysts → worms grow in dog gut → eggs passed in dog faeces → sheep (or children) swallow eggs → cysts develop in liver/lungs → cycle continues when dogs eat infected organs.",["Definitive host: dog (and other canines); intermediate: sheep, cattle, goats; humans accidental dead-end host.","Adult: 3–6 mm, scolex with hooks and 3–4 proglottids.","Egg ingested → oncosphere → portal vein → liver (60–70%), lung, other organs → hydatid cyst.","Control: deworm dogs, prevent dogs eating offal, hygiene."],"Hydatid cycle: 'Dog ↔ Sheep; Human is a dead end'."],
"Echinococcus granulosus is a small cestode (3–6 mm) with a pyriform scolex bearing four suckers and a rostellum with two rows of hooklets, a neck and three or four proglottids (the last gravid). Life cycle: definitive hosts — dogs, wolves, jackals, in whose small intestine adult worms live in large numbers; gravid proglottids or eggs (indistinguishable from Taenia eggs) are passed in faeces and contaminate pastures, water and the dog's fur. Intermediate hosts — sheep (most important), cattle, goats, pigs, camels — and accidentally humans ingest eggs; the hexacanth embryo (oncosphere) hatches in the duodenum, penetrates the intestinal wall, enters portal venules and is carried to the liver (main filter), lungs or, via systemic circulation, to brain, bones, kidneys, spleen and other organs, where it develops over months to years into a hydatid cyst with germinal layer producing brood capsules and protoscolices. When dogs eat raw offal of infected sheep, protoscolices evaginate and develop into adult worms in 6–7 weeks, completing the cycle. Humans are dead-end hosts. Echinococcus multilocularis (foxes) causes alveolar echinococcosis.",
[["Hosts",[["Host","Role","Stage"],["Dog","Definitive","Adult worm in intestine"],["Sheep, cattle","Intermediate (natural)","Hydatid cyst"],["Human","Accidental intermediate (dead end)","Hydatid cyst"]]],
["Life cycle steps",["Eggs in dog faeces","Ingestion by sheep/human","Oncosphere → portal vein → liver/lung","Hydatid cyst with protoscolices","Dog eats infected offal → adult worm"]]],
{c:"Breaking the dog–sheep cycle by deworming dogs and safe disposal of offal is the key to controlling hydatid disease."});

Z("micro8",20,"m",["Schistosomes (blood flukes) are trematodes that live in blood vessels; S. haematobium affects the bladder (haematuria, bladder cancer), S. mansoni and S. japonicum affect the intestine and liver. They are acquired by skin contact with freshwater containing cercariae from snails.","Microscopic swimmers (cercariae) released by snails bore into the skin of people wading in water, then live as pairs in veins, laying spiked eggs that injure tissues.",["Sexes separate; male holds female in gynaecophoric canal; live in venous plexuses.","S. haematobium: vesical plexus; terminal-spined eggs in urine; haematuria, bladder fibrosis, squamous cell carcinoma.","S. mansoni: lateral-spined eggs; S. japonicum: small lateral knob; intestinal and hepatosplenic disease, periportal (pipestem) fibrosis, portal hypertension.","Diagnosis: eggs in urine/stool, biopsy, serology; treatment praziquantel. Swimmer's itch; Katayama fever."],"Haematobium = 'Hematuria, terminal spine'; Mansoni = 'Lateral spine, liver'."],
"Schistosoma (blood flukes) are dioecious trematodes; the male has a gynaecophoric canal in which the slender female lies. Species: S. haematobium (Africa, Middle East; vesical and pelvic venous plexus; eggs with terminal spine passed in urine), S. mansoni (Africa, South America; inferior mesenteric veins; lateral spine; faeces), S. japonicum (East Asia; superior mesenteric veins; small lateral knob; faeces; most eggs), S. intercalatum, S. mekongi. A focus of S. haematobium existed in Gimvi, Maharashtra (India). Life cycle: eggs passed in urine or faeces hatch in freshwater releasing miracidia, which penetrate specific snail intermediate hosts (Bulinus, Biomphalaria, Oncomelania) and multiply asexually (sporocysts) producing fork-tailed cercariae; cercariae penetrate human skin during bathing, swimming or farming, lose tails to become schistosomula, migrate via lungs to the portal system, mature, pair and migrate to their final venous plexuses, where females lay eggs; eggs penetrate vessel walls and tissues to reach the bladder or bowel lumen; many are trapped in tissues. Pathogenesis: granulomatous inflammation and fibrosis around trapped eggs (Th2, eosinophils). Clinical: cercarial dermatitis (swimmer's itch); acute schistosomiasis (Katayama fever — fever, urticaria, eosinophilia); chronic — urinary (S. haematobium: terminal haematuria, bladder polyps, calcification, ureteric obstruction, hydronephrosis, squamous cell carcinoma of bladder, genital schistosomiasis), intestinal and hepatosplenic (S. mansoni, S. japonicum: diarrhoea, colonic polyps, periportal 'pipestem' (Symmers) fibrosis, presinusoidal portal hypertension with varices and splenomegaly but preserved liver function), pulmonary hypertension, CNS involvement (S. japonicum — brain). Diagnosis: eggs in urine (midday, terminal urine, filtration) or stool (Kato–Katz), rectal or bladder biopsy, egg hatching test, serology (antibodies, circulating anodic/cathodic antigens), eosinophilia, imaging. Treatment: praziquantel. Control: snail control, sanitation, safe water, mass treatment.",
[["Species",[["Feature","S. haematobium","S. mansoni","S. japonicum"],["Venous plexus","Vesical","Inferior mesenteric","Superior mesenteric"],["Egg","Terminal spine","Lateral spine","Small lateral knob"],["Specimen","Urine","Stool","Stool"],["Snail","Bulinus","Biomphalaria","Oncomelania"],["Disease","Urinary, bladder cancer","Intestinal, hepatosplenic","Intestinal, hepatosplenic, CNS"]]]],
{c:"Schistosomiasis causes chronic granulomatous disease around eggs; identification of egg morphology in urine or stool and praziquantel treatment are central to its control."});
